  "notes": "Def Jam's first R&B signing: G-funk-adjacent party R&B and slow jams, 1995–2002. The research reads \"56–104 straight; ballads 56–65 half-time\", and the party half is the one encoded. The stated ballad top of 65 fixes the straight complement at 66–104 exactly, and the mode is 104, the sourced tempo of the signature record, because that is the half whose kit the entry documents. Three drum deltas come from the sample lineage: the record is built on \"an enhanced **Teddy Riley drum sample** taken from Slick Rick's 'Children's Story'\", so the swing goes to 0.60 and the kick carries a real syncopation and tresillo lean; \"hand-claps doubling the snare\" is a clap stack with a tight offset and ghost activity on the `a` slots; \"16th hats\" run continuous at a 0.52 fill density with the open hat pinned to `4&`. The identifying parameter is the bass: the record \"interpolates the bassline of Bob James's 'Nautilus' — a walking, sliding electric-bass figure that is the record's hook\", so `followRootsProb` falls to 0.4, `glideProb` is 0.3 and the cell shapes walk through the pentatonic. Harmony is \"two-chord loops with a talk-sung baritone lead\" — `maxChords` 2, a long duration range, a triad-heavy spread; \"party records use call-and-response chants\" is `structure` `call_response`, and \"note-density low, phrasing conversational\" is the 2–6 density in the lowest melody register in this batch. ⚠ The research marks the key claim `[LOW-CONF]`, so the keys are lane-level. ⚠ The other INFERRED parameters are every velocity and density number and the countermelody.",
